20:27:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You make a very fair point. It’s absolutely true that the stereotype of the slow-moving, clock-milking plumber (or contractor in general) is deeply ingrained in pop culture. From sitcom jokes to everyday complaints between neighbors, that reputation definitely didn't just appear out of thin air—it comes from decades of homeowners experiencing the exact same frustrations you have. When a stereotype becomes that much of a "common knowledge" joke, it's usually because of a perfect storm of how the industry used to operate, combined with the nature of the work itself. Here is why that stereotype became so prevalent: **1. The hourly billing trap** For a long time, almost all plumbers charged by the hour. Anytime someone is being paid by the hour, the customer is naturally going to watch the clock. If the plumber takes a phone call, goes to their truck to find a tool, or chats for a few minutes, the homeowner feels like they are literally watching their money go down the drain. This billing structure naturally breeds suspicion. **2. The "invisible" work** A lot of plumbing involves things that don't look like active work to an observer. Waiting 15 minutes for a system to fully drain, waiting for PVC glue to cure, or driving to a supply house because a house has outdated, non-standard pipes all look like "wasting time" to a homeowner who just wants their sink fixed. **3. The asymmetry of knowledge** Because most people don't know the intricacies of plumbing, we are completely at the mercy of the person doing the job. When we don't understand *why* something is taking so long, it's very easy (and completely logical) to assume we are being taken advantage of. **4. The squeaky wheel effect** People rarely talk about the plumber who showed up on time, fixed the toilet in 20 minutes, charged a fair price, and left. But if a plumber shows up three hours late and charges $400 for a simple fix, that homeowner is going to tell everyone they know. The bad apples in the industry have done a lot of heavy lifting to create and maintain that stereotype. So, your cynicism is entirely justified by the history of the industry. However, because homeowners got so fed up with exactly what you are describing, the industry has had to evolve. That widespread frustration is exactly why **flat-rate, upfront pricing** has become so popular in recent years. Good plumbing companies realized they had to fight that exact stereotype to win customers' trust. You don't have to trust that a plumber won't milk the clock—you can just remove the clock from the equation entirely. If you refuse to hire anyone who charges by the hour, you completely eliminate their ability to live up to that stereotype.
